# THE FUSIONS WHOSE PARTS CARRY A CONVERSION NOBODY SIZED. One row per such fusion, shaped like
# the two elimination suspensions beside it so the union stays a union.
#
# ⭐⭐⭐ AN UNSIZED CONVERSION LIFTS THE SUM FOR THE SAME REASON AN UNSIZED ELIMINATION DOES, and
#    the schema already made that argument about the other one: "an entry whose `quantity` takes
#    its absent branch is a composer who found the overlap and could not size it, so the sum is
#    lifted exactly where the elimination was filed." A factor is the other multiplier in
#    `Σ converted parts`. If nobody sized it, the converted part is not a number and the sum it
#    feeds is not owed.
#
# ⛔⛔ AND WITHOUT THIS THE EXCLUSION IN composition/converted.sqlc WOULD SILENTLY UNDER-COUNT.
#    That relation drops a part whose factor is typed-absent, because converting it by one would
#    assert the units agree, which is the claim the filer declined to make. A dropped part in a
#    sum that still owes equality is a shortfall the checker invented. This is the ground that
#    keeps the two consistent.
#
# ⚠️ THE POPULATION IS ONE AND IT IS ALREADY SUSPENDED ON ANOTHER GROUND.
#    `every-unsized-conversion/shift-capacity` files its elimination search as `unmeasured` too,
#    so no verdict moves today. Recorded plainly rather than dressed up: this makes structural a
#    thing that is currently true by luck, which is the same repair as every CHECK in the DDL.
-- asrt:Part/asrt:factor taking its pm:absent branch, as a suspension of the composed sum.
SELECT p.composition, p.composed_layer,
       NULL::text AS quantity,
       'the conversion was filed and could not be sized' AS suspended_because,
       p.factor_absent::text AS note
FROM (
    :compose(composition/parts.sqlc)
) p
WHERE p.factor_absent IS NOT NULL
